  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "d \\mid b\\,c - 1,",
    "plain": "Osculators are the multipliers behind quick divisibility tests. The parent entry shows that Bézout coefficients are the canonical osculators for the base-$10$ situation. This leaf generalizes to an **arbitrary base** $b$ coprime to $d$: the same Bézout argument produces $c$ with $b\\,c \\equiv 1 \\pmod d$. So every base coprime to $d$ admits an osculator, and it is exactly the modular inverse of $b$ mod $d$.",
